	<h2>Faiths of the Crusades</h2>
	<p>The crusades against the demons of the Abyss are fundamentally a faith-driven conflict between the forces and
		agents of the divine and profane. While faith in a deity is not required, it is highly recommended, and many of the
		allies and resources encountered in this campaign have ties to the deities of golarion, even if their relationship
		is rather complicated. The major faiths featured in this campaign can be grouped as follows:</p>
	<ul style="list-style-type:circle">
		<li style="list-style-type: none;"><b>Click to Reveal the Faiths of the Crusades:</b></li>
		<li style="list-style-type: none; display: none;"><b>Click to Apostatize the Faiths of the Crusades:</b></li>
		<li style="display: none;"><b>Crusading Faiths:</b> <i>(Abadar, Aroden, Asmodeus, Iomedae, Sarenrae, Shelyn,
				Torag)</i><br>
			These faiths and churches actively send crusaders and paladins to fight the Abyss, and are the most appropriate
			choices of faith for an adventurer. The crusade is championed primarily by Iomedae and her forces, <i>Calling</i>
			of Queen Galifrey to lead the Kingdom of Mendev and call for the first crusade being one of her first acts as the
			Inheritor. Shortly after she called Ragathiels Herald, the Hand of the Inheritor (formerly the Hand of Vengeance)
			to become her herald, and lead the Pyralisia, Terendelev, and her host of angels in personally defending Golarion
			from the swarm of Deskari, as Aroden had done in the flesh three hundred years prior. The faitfhul of Abadar,
			Sarenrae, Shelyn, and Torag also contribute funds and forces, and form the backbone of the logistics, restoration,
			redemption, and engineering aspects of the crusades. Those who still follow Aroden are often viewed as stubborn or
			old fashioned, taking up the banner but not the name of the inheritor and clinging to a dead god. Meanwhile, the
			devil-worshipping church of Asmodeus takes great care in sending paladins and inquisitors alike to contain the
			demonic threat, and their skill and zeal are appreciated, though they are never fully accepted or trusted by the
			other crusaders.</li>
		<li style="display: none;"><b>Sarkorian Faiths:</b> <i>(Desna, Gorum, Gozreh, Lamashtu, Pharasma, Pulura)</i><br>
			While an Ustalavan scholar once pejoratively referred to the faiths of the northern Sarkorian peoples as the
			worship of "a hundred hungry gods," his statement failed both to innumerate the vastness of their pantheon or
			highlight the complexity of their worship. Their polytheistic worship varied clan to clan and covered the gamut
			from deities to conceptual ideas, including venerating outsiders, fae, and spirits of all kinds. Each clan also
			adopted personal patrons for their druidic, shamanistic, god-calling, and witchcraft practices, with these patrons
			often granting supernatural powers. However, the most prominent of these faiths still make for good patron deities
			for adventurers, as they are the most involved with local and historical peoples and places, and have a vested
			interest in the wounded lands. Desna is particularly prominent, being responsible for the hope and dreams of all
			people, and features heavily in this campaign. Followers of Gorum seek warfare, and there are few wars as
			promising of relentless battles as crusades. Pharasma as the goddess of death and fate is extremely interested in
			preserving the river of souls, though she does not ultimately care whether souls are bound for either Heaven or
			the Pitt. Pulura is the goddess of the north star and the Aurora, and the house of her worship is lost in the
			wounded lands, with her faithful eager to recover it. Meanwhile Gozreh and the green faith both feature
			prominently among the druidic traditions of the land, as does Lamashtu's interest in the particular beasts unique
			to this frontier.</li>
		<li style="display: none;"><b>Other Involved Faiths:</b> <i>(Cayden Cailean, Erastil, Ragathiel)</i><br>
			These religions hold a prominent and involved role amongst the crusades, even if their churches do not directly
			send money and troops in support, and these are a pretty good choice for adventurers, particularly those who
			explore the Worldwound for reasons other than duty. Ragathiel was an early and committed party to the crusades,
			granting his herald to Iomedae to further aid the war effort, but he lacks both reach and influence to remain
			prominent, and his followers tend to fight recklessly to the death rather than coordinating a strong resistance.
			The faithful from among Cayden Calean and Erastil similarly make up fair portion of crusaders, but those that join
			do so out of personal conviction rather than a religious mandate.</li>
		<li style="display: none;"><b>Demonic Faith:</b> <i>(Baphomet, Deskari, Nocticula, Lamashtu)</i><br>
			While these faiths are generally not appropriate for adventurers to worship, these are the most prominent demon
			lords involved in their war against the crusaders. Deskari is the most prominent and dangerous, with his cultists
			spreading his faith swiftly through the superstitious and polytheistic faiths of Sarkoris hundreds of years before
			the opening of the Worldwound, and amassing enough power to manifest an avatar physically on Golarion at the lake
			of Mists and Veils, only to be slain by the ascendent hand of Aroden himself. Since then, he continued to corrupt
			the lands and people here, waiting until the death of Aroden to sweep across Golarion in the form of a demonic
			plague, kicking off the first crusade. A few decades ago, Baphomet took interest in these affairs, perhaps through
			an unholy alliance, and he set his faithful to infiltrate, corrupt, and mislead crusaders, leading to the witch
			hunts of the third crusade. Lamashtu, mother of monsters, meanwhile nurtures the corrupting nature of the
			worldwound, lovingly birthing new abominations, and her feral worshippers often seek both her power and her
			protection. Nocticula meanwhile loves engaging in the politics and shadow games of mortals, and the worldwound
			gives her a chance to play the same game with demons and gods, as well as their easily seduced followers. Her
			spies pry secrets from all sides and use them with surgical precision to cause as much death and destruction as
			possible. While other demons also have worshippers of their own, these demon lords are at the top of their food
			chains.</li>
	</ul>
</section>